[
  {
    "pkheadingid": 703,
    "bookid": 44,
    "headingType": 2,
    "headingTitle": "Definition",
    "headingPosition": 1,
    "headingid": 466,
    "contributions": [
      {
        "pkcontributionId": 105442,
        "bookId": 44,
        "chapterId": 150,
        "headingId": 466,
        "headingText": "\r\n<p>Gardner fibroma (GF) is a benign fibroblastic/myofibroblastic, plaque-like lesion composed of dense collagen bundles with sparse fibroblasts, usually associated with familial adenomatous polyposis (FAP).</p>\r\n",
        "timestamp": "2023-03-15T11:26:23"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  },
  {
    "pkheadingid": 704,
    "bookid": 44,
    "headingType": 2,
    "headingTitle": "ICD-O coding",
    "headingPosition": 2,
    "headingid": 467,
    "contributions": [
      {
        "pkcontributionId": 105443,
        "bookId": 44,
        "chapterId": 150,
        "headingId": 467,
        "headingText": "\r\n<p>8810/0 Gardner fibroma</p>\r\n",
        "timestamp": "2023-03-15T11:26:23"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  },
  {
    "pkheadingid": 705,
    "bookid": 44,
    "headingType": 2,
    "headingTitle": "ICD-11 coding",
    "headingPosition": 3,
    "headingid": 468,
    "contributions": [
      {
        "pkcontributionId": 105444,
        "bookId": 44,
        "chapterId": 150,
        "headingId": 468,
        "headingText": "<p >EE6Y &amp; XH7GT0 Other specified fibromatous disorders of skin and soft tissue &amp; Gardner fibroma</p>",
        "timestamp": "2023-03-31T14:04:11"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  },
  {
    "pkheadingid": 706,
    "bookid": 44,
    "headingType": 2,
    "headingTitle": "Related terminology",
    "headingPosition": 4,
    "headingid": 469,
    "contributions": [
      {
        "pkcontributionId": 105445,
        "bookId": 44,
        "chapterId": 150,
        "headingId": 469,
        "headingText": "\r\n<p><em>Acceptable:</em> Gardner-associated fibroma.</p>\r\n<p><em>Not recommended:</em> desmoid precursor lesion; nuchal fibroma; collagenosis nuchae.</p>\r\n",
        "timestamp": "2023-03-15T11:26:23"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  },
  {
    "pkheadingid": 707,
    "bookid": 44,
    "headingType": 2,
    "headingTitle": "Subtype(s)",
    "headingPosition": 5,
    "headingid": 470,
    "contributions": [
      {
        "pkcontributionId": 105446,
        "bookId": 44,
        "chapterId": 150,
        "headingId": 470,
        "headingText": "\r\n<p>None</p>\r\n",
        "timestamp": "2023-03-15T11:26:23"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  },
  {
    "pkheadingid": 708,
    "bookid": 44,
    "headingType": 2,
    "headingTitle": "Localization",
    "headingPosition": 6,
    "headingid": 471,
    "contributions": [
      {
        "pkcontributionId": 105447,
        "bookId": 44,
        "chapterId": 150,
        "headingId": 471,
        "headingText": "\r\n<p>GF arises in the superficial and deep soft tissues of the torso (most commonly in the back or paraspinal region), head/neck, and rarely other non-visceral sites (e.g. the mesentery) {11342777; 17325483}.</p>\r\n",
        "timestamp": "2023-03-15T11:26:23"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  },
  {
    "pkheadingid": 709,
    "bookid": 44,
    "headingType": 2,
    "headingTitle": "Clinical features",
    "headingPosition": 7,
    "headingid": 472,
    "contributions": [
      {
        "pkcontributionId": 105448,
        "bookId": 44,
        "chapterId": 150,
        "headingId": 472,
        "headingText": "\r\n<p>GF is a painless plaque/mass {11342777; 17325483}.</p>\r\n",
        "timestamp": "2023-03-15T11:26:23"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  },
  {
    "pkheadingid": 710,
    "bookid": 44,
    "headingType": 2,
    "headingTitle": "Epidemiology",
    "headingPosition": 8,
    "headingid": 473,
    "contributions": [
      {
        "pkcontributionId": 105449,
        "bookId": 44,
        "chapterId": 150,
        "headingId": 473,
        "headingText": "<p>GF is usually diagnosed in young children (aged &lt;&nbsp;10&nbsp;years), including neonates, but it can be seen in older children {11342777; 17325483; 28018803}. In contrast, nuchal-type fibroma, which has many overlapping features with GF, occurs mainly in middle-aged men, and as many as 50% of nuchal-type fibroma patients have diabetes mellitus {7872429}.</p>",
        "timestamp": "2023-05-10T15:27:06"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  },
  {
    "pkheadingid": 711,
    "bookid": 44,
    "headingType": 2,
    "headingTitle": "Etiology",
    "headingPosition": 9,
    "headingid": 474,
    "contributions": [
      {
        "pkcontributionId": 105450,
        "bookId": 44,
        "chapterId": 150,
        "headingId": 474,
        "headingText": "\r\n<p>Unknown</p>\r\n",
        "timestamp": "2023-03-15T11:26:23"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  },
  {
    "pkheadingid": 712,
    "bookid": 44,
    "headingType": 2,
    "headingTitle": "Pathogenesis",
    "headingPosition": 10,
    "headingid": 475,
    "contributions": [
      {
        "pkcontributionId": 105451,
        "bookId": 44,
        "chapterId": 150,
        "headingId": 475,
        "headingText": "\r\n<p>GFs are associated with <em>APC</em> germline mutations and are often the first evidence of FAP {17325483; 26840078; 25074465}. Biallelic inactivation of <em>APC</em> has been reported in GFs, with documented germline mutations accompanied by somatic mutations of <em>APC</em> in tumour tissue {25074465}.</p>\r\n",
        "timestamp": "2023-03-15T11:26:23"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  },
  {
    "pkheadingid": 719,
    "bookid": 44,
    "headingType": 2,
    "headingTitle": "Macroscopic appearance",
    "headingPosition": 11,
    "headingid": 482,
    "contributions": [
      {
        "pkcontributionId": 105452,
        "bookId": 44,
        "chapterId": 150,
        "headingId": 482,
        "headingText": "\r\n<p>GF is poorly circumscribed, rubbery, and tan-white; it varies widely in size {11342777; 17325483}.</p>\r\n",
        "timestamp": "2023-03-15T11:26:23"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  },
  {
    "pkheadingid": 713,
    "bookid": 44,
    "headingType": 2,
    "headingTitle": "Histopathology",
    "headingPosition": 12,
    "headingid": 476,
    "contributions": [
      {
        "pkcontributionId": 105453,
        "bookId": 44,
        "chapterId": 150,
        "headingId": 476,
        "headingText": "\r\n<p>GF exhibits haphazardly arranged coarse collagen bundles interspersed with bland spindled fibroblasts and prominent clefts (cracking artefact). A helpful diagnostic feature is the entrapment of fat and neurovascular bundles at the edge of the lesion. These tumours are CD34-positive, SMA-negative, and often show nuclear &beta;-catenin staining, reflective of their association with FAP {11342777; 17325483; 26840078}.</p>\r\n",
        "timestamp": "2023-03-15T11:26:23"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  },
  {
    "pkheadingid": 714,
    "bookid": 44,
    "headingType": 2,
    "headingTitle": "Cytology",
    "headingPosition": 13,
    "headingid": 477,
    "contributions": [
      {
        "pkcontributionId": 105454,
        "bookId": 44,
        "chapterId": 150,
        "headingId": 477,
        "headingText": "\r\n<p>Not clinically relevant</p>\r\n",
        "timestamp": "2023-03-15T11:26:23"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  },
  {
    "pkheadingid": 715,
    "bookid": 44,
    "headingType": 2,
    "headingTitle": "Diagnostic molecular pathology",
    "headingPosition": 14,
    "headingid": 478,
    "contributions": [
      {
        "pkcontributionId": 105455,
        "bookId": 44,
        "chapterId": 150,
        "headingId": 478,
        "headingText": "\r\n<p>GF is associated with germline and somatic <em>APC</em> mutations {25074465}. An aggressive phenotype has been described with a specific c.4666dupA (p.T1556NfsX3) <em>APC</em> variant {30696621}. The molecular abnormalities in nuchal-type fibroma have no clinical relevance.</p>\r\n",
        "timestamp": "2023-03-15T11:26:23"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  },
  {
    "pkheadingid": 718,
    "bookid": 44,
    "headingType": 2,
    "headingTitle": "Essential and desirable diagnostic criteria",
    "headingPosition": 15,
    "headingid": 481,
    "contributions": [
      {
        "pkcontributionId": 105456,
        "bookId": 44,
        "chapterId": 150,
        "headingId": 481,
        "headingText": "\r\n<p><em>Essential:</em> a poorly defined, bland and paucicellular, densely collagenized, CD34-positive tumour occurring in a child; often associated with FAP and <em>APC</em> mutations.</p>\r\n",
        "timestamp": "2023-03-15T11:26:23"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  },
  {
    "pkheadingid": 716,
    "bookid": 44,
    "headingType": 2,
    "headingTitle": "Staging",
    "headingPosition": 16,
    "headingid": 479,
    "contributions": [
      {
        "pkcontributionId": 105457,
        "bookId": 44,
        "chapterId": 150,
        "headingId": 479,
        "headingText": "\r\n<p>Not clinically relevant</p>\r\n",
        "timestamp": "2023-03-15T11:26:23"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  },
  {
    "pkheadingid": 717,
    "bookid": 44,
    "headingType": 2,
    "headingTitle": "Prognosis and prediction",
    "headingPosition": 17,
    "headingid": 480,
    "contributions": [
      {
        "pkcontributionId": 105458,
        "bookId": 44,
        "chapterId": 150,
        "headingId": 480,
        "headingText": "\r\n<p>GF is benign, but its occasional transformation into desmoid fibromatosis (in 20% of cases), as well as its association with FAP, should prompt further screening of the patient and their relatives {17325483; 25064609}.</p>",
        "timestamp": "2023-03-15T11:26:23"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  }
]